    id: 80,
    title: "My Safe Florida Home and Impact Windows: How the Grant Works",
    slug: "my-safe-florida-home-grant-impact-windows",
    excerpt:
      "My Safe Florida Home is a state grant of up to $10,000, not a store discount. Here is how it fits an impact window project, and when work must wait.",
    content: `<p>My Safe Florida Home is the program South Florida homeowners bring up in the same sentence as financing, and the two are not the same thing. The program is a Florida Department of Financial Services grant. On qualifying homesteaded homes it can be worth up to $10,000 toward hurricane-hardening work after a program inspection and a written approval. It is not a coupon we apply at the bottom of a proposal, and it is not one of the lender plans on our <a href="/financing/">financing</a> page.</p>
<p>The rules are specific enough that a casual summary causes real problems. The version we follow, and that we ask customers to confirm because the program changes, is this: the home is a site-built house with an original building permit dated before January 1, 2008; the applicant is working through the program's own inspection; and, except for low-income applicants, the insured dwelling value is at or below $700,000. Starting the work before written grant approval can disqualify the project. That last rule is the one that hurts people who are trying to beat hurricane season. Current details live at MySafeFLHome.com. If the site and this article ever disagree, the program site wins.</p>
<h2>What the grant is for, and what it does not replace</h2>
<p>The inspection comes first. The program is trying to see which improvements the house is eligible to fund, which can include opening protection and can also include roof-related work, depending on what the inspection finds and what the current cycle is paying for. Impact windows and doors are a common use when opening protection is the recommended improvement. They are not automatically the improvement the program will fund on every house. If the inspection prioritizes the roof, a window order does not override that.</p>
<p>We can measure the openings, explain <a href="/services/impact-windows/">impact windows</a> and doors, and price a scope. We should not tell you to tear out the old glass while an application is pending. A measure and a written proposal are planning. Demolition and installation are the work the program is warning you not to start early. If you need the project to move on a lender plan instead of a grant, or after a grant reduces the balance, that is a financing conversation and it should be labeled as one. The <a href="/blog/financing-options-impact-windows-south-florida/">financing overview</a> covers those plans. Stacking them is sometimes possible: the grant reduces what you finance. It does not make the lender terms disappear, and it does not make you eligible.</p>
<h2>Eligibility is a house question</h2>
<p>Condos, townhomes, and newer houses run into the rules faster than a 1970s block house on a homesteaded lot. The program language we rely on says site-built, permitted before January 1, 2008, homesteaded, and under the insured-value cap unless the low-income exception applies. If your house was permitted in 2012, or the dwelling value is above the cap and you are not in the low-income path, we will not build a proposal that pretends the grant is coming. You might still want the windows. You would pay for them without that grant.</p>
<p>Geography does not create eligibility. Miami-Dade, Broward, and Palm Beach homeowners all ask, and the HVHZ does not confer a grant. The form the insurer uses later, OIR-B1-1802, is also a different document from the program inspection. One can help your premium after the glass is in. The other is how the state decides whether it will help pay for the work. People merge them because both inspectors walk around the house with a clipboard. Keep the folders separate.</p>
<h2>How we sequence a grant project</h2>
<p>The safe sequence is: confirm that the current cycle is open and that the house looks eligible, complete the program inspection, receive written approval that names the work, then order and install. Product choice still has to satisfy the building department. In Miami-Dade and Broward that means HVHZ approvals. A grant does not waive the permit or the Notice of Acceptance. It also does not pick the brand. We still specify PGT, CGI, ES Windows, or Custom Window Systems based on the openings, and the approval documents still have to match.</p>
<p>Read the approval before you celebrate the full $10,000. The figure people repeat is the maximum, not a promise that every application is funded at the cap or that every opening on the house is covered. If the grant covers part of the scope, decide in writing whether you are doing only that part or whether you are paying for the rest. Partial opening protection can also limit the insurance credit later, which is a reason to see the whole opening list even when the grant is aimed at a smaller list.</p>
<p>Treat the grant as helpful when it is approved, and as absent until that letter exists. Keep copies of the program inspection, the written approval, the city permit, and the product approvals in one folder. You will want that folder for the installer, for the program if they ask how the money was used, and later for the insurance form. We can add our installation documents. We cannot replace a program letter you never received. If a cycle closes or the rules shift while you are waiting, pause the order rather than hoping the old brochure still applies.</p>
<p>If you want a scope you can take into that process, or a scope that does not depend on it, request a <a href="/get-estimate/">free estimate</a> or call (754) 600-4876. The office is Florida Impact Windows &amp; Doors, 3000 Stirling Rd, Hollywood, FL 33021.</p>`,
